                <pb facs="0255" n="181" sameAs="frus:frus1941-43/pg181"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>No official record of this conversation has been found. In the Stimson diary for
                    the period January 8–11, the Secretary of War noted that the British had
                    advanced “some propositions for the creation of a future strategic joint
                    organization” which Stimson considered “completely wrong.” (See the memorandum
                    WW–8, <hi rendition="#italic">post</hi>, <ref target="#pg217">p. 217</ref>.) The
                    diary note concludes with the following sentence: “Marshall told me over the
                    telephone Sunday evening [January 11] that he had been in conference for two
                    hours with the President that afternoon and had represented strongly the
                    objections to this organization.”</p>
            </div>
